findings.description:
Both sibling resolvers changed in this PR guard the null payload; this one lost the guard. The diff's own evidence establishes the GitHub behavior: ProjectStackResolverTest keeps the comment "Submodules, directories, and oversized files come back without base64 content", RepoSettingsResolverTest keeps "The contents endpoint can answer without an inline payload (e.g. an over-size blob); decoding null would NPE, so it must be handled as 'nothing readable here'", and ProjectStackResolver.java adds `if (content == null || content.isBlank())` plus `if (content == null)`. RepoSettingsResolver mirrors it. InstructionsResolver instead does, unguarded:

        var content = file.getContent();
        cache.put(cacheKey, new CachedInstructions(content, path, clock.getAsLong() + CACHE_TTL_MS));
        ...
        log.info("Using instructions file: {} ({} bytes)", path, content.length());

For an oversized-but-type-"file" entry (`isFile()` is true, `getContent()` is null), the cache is populated with the null content first, then `content.length()` throws an NPE that the broad `catch (RuntimeException | IOException _)` swallows as "Instructions file not found". The fallback continues, but the poisoned entry stays live: on the next `resolve` for the same repo within `CACHE_TTL_MS`, the cache hit path returns `Optional.of(new ResolvedInstructions(null, path))`, handing callers null instructions. Both the null and blank cases need to be treated as an unusable candidate before the cache write. Verify that hub4j's `GHContent.getContent()` returns null for such entries (the above comments say the contents API answers without an inline payload).

findings.suggestion_new:
// hub4j's getContent() already decodes the base64 payload.
var content = file.getContent();
if (content == null || content.isBlank()) {
  continue;
}

findings.description:
The test stubs the same call twice:

    stubFile(YML, "review:\n  ignored-files:\n    - \"gen/**\"\n");
    doReturn(content("review:\n  ignored-files:\n    - \"other/**\"\n"))
        .when(repository)
        .getFileContent(YML, DEFAULT_BRANCH);

In Mockito the later `doReturn` on the identical method+matchers replaces the first, so the first resolve returns "other/**" — and the assertion was changed from `java.util.List.of("gen/**")` to `java.util.List.of("other/**")` instead of the stub order being fixed. Worse, the migration made `setUp` stub `gitHub.getRepository(anyString())` to return one shared `repository` mock regardless of owner/repo, so `resolve(OWNER, "other", ...)` hits the same stub and the two resolves produce identical results whether the cache is keyed per repository, keyed globally, or disabled entirely. The test's name promises per-repository keying; it now can no longer detect a cache-key regression. Give `gitHub.getRepository` distinct repository mocks per owner/name (or per-name stubbing) and restore the `gen/**` / `other/**` expectations.

The new code fetches the repository inside the per-path loop:

    for (String path : fallbackChain) {
      try {
        var repository =
            clientProvider.getInstallationClient(installationId).getRepository(owner + "/" + repo);

hub4j's `GitHub.getRepository(name)` eagerly performs `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}`, so every fallback candidate costs its own API round trip for data that does not vary with the path. For a repo with none of the instruction files, that is 5 chain entries × 1 metadata call per resolve, where one lookup hoisted above the loop suffices. The identical pattern was added in RepoSettingsResolver (`fetchAndParse` calls `getRepository(owner + "/" + repo)` per CONFIG_FILE_CHAIN entry) and ProjectStackResolver (per MANIFEST_PATHS entry), and in `GitHubIssuesProvider.read` once per candidate issue (each of up to MAX_CANDIDATES reads re-resolves the repository). Please verify that `GitHub.getRepository` is indeed an eager HTTP call in the resolved hub4j version; if so, hoist the client+repository resolution to once per resolve call. Note the hoist moves a throwing call outside the current per-path try; keep the failure soft by wrapping it once.

`doesNotFetchAnythingWhenTheFeatureIsDisabled` now reads:

      verifyNoInteractions(repository);
      verifyNoInteractions(repository);

The old pair was `verifyNoInteractions(prClient)` + `verifyNoInteractions(authClient)`. The migration replaced both with the identical `repository` check, so the second line is a no-op and nothing asserts that the disabled path leaves the client machinery (`clientProvider` / `gitHub`) untouched. Duplicate line visible verbatim in the diff; restore a second, distinct verification or delete the duplicate.

The test still builds and asserts on a MIME-wrapped payload:

    var wrapped =
        Base64.getMimeEncoder(60, "\n".getBytes(java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets.UTF_8))
            .encodeToString(content.getBytes(java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
    assertTrue(wrapped.contains("\n"));

but then stubs the resolver with the plain variable: `doReturn(makeFileContent(content)).when(repository).getFileContent(...)`. The wrapped value never reaches the resolver, production no longer MIME-decodes anything (it consumes hub4j's already-decoded string), and the method name claims coverage that no longer exists. The scaffolding is dead; either delete it and rename the test to what it actually verifies, or acknowledge in the name that it now only round-trips decoded content.
